'''utter''' (ut′ėr), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ME. ''utter'', ''uttur'', ''uttre'', < AS. ''ūtera'', ''ūtterra'', ''ūttra'', ''ȳtra'' = OFries. ''ūtere'' = OHG. ''ūzero'', ''ūzzero'' = Icel. ''ytri'' = Sw. ''yttre'' = Dan. ''ydre'', adj.; cf. early ME. ''utter'', < AS. ''ūtor'', ''ūttor'' = OS. ''ūtar'' = OHG. ''ūzar'', ''ūzer'', MHG. ''ūzer'', G. ''äusser'', adv. and prep.; compar. of AS. ''ūt'', etc., out: see ''out'', and cf. ''outer''<sup>1</sup>, of which ''utter'' is a doublet.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1†.''' That is or lies on the exterior or outside; outer.
{{smaller block|<poem>ȝomon [yeoman] vssher be-fore the dore,
In ''vttur'' chambur lies on the flore.</poem>
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 316.}}
To the Bridge’s ''utter'' gate I came.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. x. 11.}}
Then he brought me forth into the ''utter'' court.
{{right|Ezek. xlvi. 21.}}
He compassed the inner City with three walls, & the ''vtter'' City with as many. {{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 56.}}}}
'''2†.''' Situated at or beyond the limits of something; remote from some center; outward; outside of any place or space.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ther lakketh nothing to thyn ''utter'' eyen
That thou nart blind.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Second Nun’s Tale, l. 498.}}
Through ''utter'' and through middle darkness borne.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iii. 16.}}}}
'''3.''' Complete; total; entire; perfect; absolute.
{{smaller block|<poem>Thy foul disgrace
And ''utter'' ruin of the house of York.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., i. 1. 254.}}
Gentlemen, ye be ''utter'' strangers to me; I know you not.
{{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim’s Progress, p. 184.}}
<poem>A low despairing cry</poem>}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Of ''utter'' misery: “Let me die!”</poem>
{{right|''Whittier'', The Witch’s Daughter.}}}}

'''4.''' Peremptory; absolute; unconditional; unqualified; final.

{{smaller block|''Utter'' refusal. {{right|''Clarendon.''}}}}

'''Utter barrister.''' {{smaller|See ''outer bar'', under ''outer''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' The extreme; the utmost.

{{smaller block|I take my leave readie to countervaile all your courtesies to the ''utter'' of my power.
{{right|''Aubrey'', Lives, Walter Raleigh.}}}}

{{smaller block|[Excessive pressure] produces an irregular indented surface, which by workmen is said to be full of ''utters.''
{{right|''O. Byrne'', Artisan’s Handbook, p. 335.}}}}

'''utter''' (ut′ėr), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''uttren'', ''outren'' (= LG. ''ütern'' = MHG. ''ūzern'', ''inzern'', G. ''äussern'' = Sw. ''yttra'' = Dan. ''ytre''), put out, utter, < AS. ''ūtor'', ''ūttor'', out, outside: see ''utter'', ''a.'' Cf. ''out'', ''v.''] '''1.''' To put out or forth; expel; emit.

{{smaller block|Who, having this inward overthrow in himself, was the more vexed that he could not ''utter'' the rage thereof upon his outward enemies. {{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, iii.}}}}

{{smaller block|He looked in vain for the sage Nicholas Vedder, with his broad face, double chin, and fair long pipe, ''uttering'' clouds of tobacco-smoke instead of idle speeches.
{{right|''Irving'', Rip van Winkle.}}}}

'''2.''' To dispose of to the public or in the way of trade; specifically, to put into circulation, as money, notes, base coin, etc.: now used only in the latter specific sense.

{{smaller block|<poem>With danger ''uttren'' we al our chaffare;
Gret prees at market maketh dere ware.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Wife of Bath’s Tale (ed. Tyrwhitt),}}
{{right|[l. 521.}}}}

{{smaller block|Marchauntes do ''utter'' . . . wares and commodities.
{{right|''Sir T. Elyot'', The Governour, iii. 30.}}}}

