{{right|''Richard the Redeless'', iii. 232.}}}}
'''2.''' Utterly.
{{smaller block|<poem>So ''utter'' empty of those excellencies
That tame authority.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', King and No King, iv. 1.}}
It ''utter'' excludes his former excuse of an allegory.
{{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 47.}}}}

'''utterable''' (ut′ėr-a̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''utter'' + ''-able.''] Capable of being uttered, pronounced, or expressed.
{{smaller block|He hath changed the ineffable name into a name ''utterable'' by man, and desirable by all the world.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), I. 53.}}}}

'''utterableness''' (ut′ėr-a̤-bl-nes), ''n.'' The character of being utterable.

'''utterance<sup>1</sup>''' (ut′ėr-ans), ''n.'' [< ''utter'' + ''-ance.''] '''1.''' The act of uttering. {{smaller|(''a'') A putting forth; disposal by sale or otherwise; circulation.}}
{{smaller block|What of our comodities haue most ''vtterance'' there, and what prices will be giuen for them.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 300.}}
But the English have so ill ''utterance'' for their warm clothes in these hot countries. ''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 95.}}
{{smaller|(''b'') The act of sounding or expressing with the voice; vocal expression; also, power of speaking; speech.}}
{{smaller block|Where so euer knowledge doth accompanie the witte, there best ''vtterance'' doth alwaies awaite vpon the tonge.
{{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster, p. 29.}}
They . . . began to speak with other tongues, as the Spirit gave them ''utterance''. Acts ii. 4.
<poem>Even as a man that in some trance hath seen
More than his wondering ''utterance'' can unfold.</poem>
{{right|''Drayton'', Idea, lvii.}}
Her Charms are dumb, they want ''Utterance''.
{{right|''Steele'', Grief A-la-Mode, iii. 1.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
'''2.''' That which is uttered or conveyed by the voice; a word or words: as, the ''utterances'' of the pulpit.
{{smaller block|<poem>I hear a sound of many languages,
The ''utterance'' of nations now no more.</poem>
{{right|''Bryant'', Earth.}}}}
{{smaller block|Their emotional ''utterances'' [those of the lower animals] are rich and various, and, when we once get the right clue to their interpretation, reveal a vast life of pleasure and pain, want and satisfaction.
{{right|''J. Sully'', Sensation and Intuition, p. 15.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Barrel-organ utterance''', the involuntary repetition of a word or phrase just uttered by the speaker or another; echolalia. See also ''recurring utterances.''—'''Recurring utterances.''' See ''recurring.''—'''Scanning utterance.''' Same as ''syllabic utterance.''—'''Staccato utterance.''' Same as ''syllabic utterance.''—'''Syllabic utterance''', a defect in speech consisting in an inability to enunciate as a whole a word of more than one syllable, in consequence of which each syllable must be sounded independently as a separate word.}}

'''utterance<sup>2</sup>†''' (ut′ėr-ans), ''n.'' [An expanded form, due to confusion with ''utter'', ''uttermost'', of *''uttrance'', ''uttraunce'', earlier ''outrance'': see ''outrance.''] The last or utmost extremity; the bitter end; death.
{{smaller block|<poem>Come fate into the list,
And champion me to the ''utterance''!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iii. 1. 72.}}}}

'''utterer''' (ut′ėr-ėr), ''n.'' [< ''utter'', ''v.'', + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who utters. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') One who disposes of, by sale or otherwise.}}
{{smaller block|''Utterers'' of fish, maintained chiefly by fishing.
{{right|''Privy Council'' (Arber’s Eng. Garner, I. 301).}}}}
